<p dir="auto">The engine has sharp edges, and the compiler works around them:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>A floor mover ignores a second request while it is still moving.</li>
<li>A teleport cancels lines a doll crossed earlier in the same tic.</li>
<li>Things far from a BSP node can land in the wrong room, so every map fits in 32,000 units.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto"><code>doomformer facts</code> checks these in about 10 s. <a href="https://github.com/mottopanikeiku/doomformer/blob/main/docs/HOW_IT_WORKS.md#engine-facts">Engine facts</a> ·
<a href="https://github.com/mottopanikeiku/doomformer/blob/main/docs/HOW_IT_WORKS.md#engine-features-it-needs">Engine features it needs</a> ·
<a href="https://github.com/mottopanikeiku/doomformer/blob/main/docs/engine_limits.md#other-ports">Other ports</a></p>
<p dir="auto">Computers in DOOM maps are not new. <a href="https://www.wad-archive.com/wad/8d0de31d8b392fc2d7fd817e933b3cf43bca799a" rel="nofollow">counter.wad</a>
counts with voodoo dolls, <a href="https://github.com/pwiecz/conway.wad">conway.wad</a> runs Game of Life, and
<a href="https://github.com/nukep/doom-calculator">Danny Spencer's calculator</a> adds numbers. New here: a compiler for neural
networks, and an exact check on every value. <a href="https://github.com/mottopanikeiku/doomformer/blob/main/docs/related_work.md">Related work</a></p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">What is checked</h2><a id="user-content-what-is-checked" aria-label="Permalink: What is checked" href="#what-is-checked"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">
  <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/mottopanikeiku/doomformer/releases/download/v0.4.0/pipeline.png"><img src="https://github.com/mottopanikeiku/doomformer/releases/download/v0.4.0/pipeline.png" alt="Pipeline: your network becomes integers and a DOOM map, ViZDoom runs it and Python reads the values back (light levels and plane heights). A NumPy integer reference, which never sees the map, computes the same values. Every value is compared with tolerance 0: EXACT or MismatchError." width="800"></a>
</p>
<p dir="auto">Every run compares each value the map exposes with NumPy. That proves the inputs that ran, nothing more. So the repo
also attacks its own check:</p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>attack</th>
<th>result</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tamper</strong> with one weight line</td>
<td>the check fails on exactly the inputs that cross it</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Fuzz</strong> 20,000 random nets at the range limits</td>
<td>0 mismatches; CI fuzzes a fresh seed on every push</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Mutate</strong> one line of a map, 1,004 times</td>
<td>297 caught; no survivor is proven harmless</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Extract</strong> the weights back out of the map, without DOOM</td>
<td>equal to the source net</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Guard</strong> every readout page with a poison value</td>
<td>a page that never runs cannot pass old values</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir="auto">The MNIST map exposes 248 values. It does not expose its 903 staircase counts and 1,351 gate comparisons. Those are
checked only through the sums they feed. Fuzzing and tests found four bugs in the compiler. All are fixed.
<a href="https://github.com/mottopanikeiku/doomformer/blob/main/docs/verification.md">Verification</a></p>
<details>
<summary><b>Who computes what</b></summary>
