        <h2 id="mindustry">Mindustry</h2>

<p><strong>Game replay:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p>Click “AI-generated transcript analysis” to view an analysis of the team discussions, strategies, etc. for each game!</p></li>
<li><p>Use the panel on the right (tabs “Claude chat”, “GPT chat” and “Agents”) to watch a team or a specific agent’s live chat and transcripts in sync with the timeline.</p></li>
<li><p>Use the + and - buttons on the map to zoom in and watch them up close.</p></li>
</ul>
<figure id="mindustry-replay"></figure>
<p>The GPTs (GPT-6.1 Sol in Codex) won 6-1 against the Claudes (Claude Opus 5.5 in Claude Code) in the 7 games. The series ran for about 9.5 hours, with game 3 being the most intense and closest at 4 hours 16 minutes. Outside of the last two games, where the Claudes were more defeated, most of the other games were very close, with the swarms adapting and iterating their strategies each game.</p>
<h3 id="mindustry-setup">The setup</h3>
<p><a href="https://github.com/Anuken/Mindustry" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mindustry</a> is an open source game by Anuken. Full credit to him for the amazing game and letting it be open source for cool experiments like this!</p>
<p>It’s a factory-building game with tower defense and real-time strategy: you mine resources, build conveyor supply chains and factories, and turn that economy into turrets and an army. In its PvP mode, which we used, two teams race to build the stronger economy and military and destroy the other team’s core.</p>
<p>This makes it a great environment for testing multi-agent dynamics. Mindustry is 2D, so it can be played easily by agents, is replayable, and is prone to adversarial pressures that can test these swarms under competitive social conditions that they may face in the real world.</p>
<p>We structured the simulated environment to have two teams of agents spawn on each side of a map, with the same resources on both sides. They play a best of N series, where you set how many games the teams play. When launching it, we set the composition of the teams (which harness + model, and how many), then let it run! The agents run in their own sandbox and the game is exposed to them through an API, so they are able to run scripts and interact with the game however they like in their own Linux environment.</p>
<div><p>Both teams have the exact same API to the game, without any special instructions given to them on how to play - neither team is given anything more than the other team.</p><p>

Multi-agent swarms, like in <a href="https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/67869394-cb91-4c12-888c-5cbd85c7814c/OpenAI-Hugging-Face%20Incident-Technical-Report.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the OpenAI and Hugging Face incident</a>, can turn simple adversarial conditions, like being in a sandbox with an unsolvable task, into a hive-minded ecology of agents that take over compute clusters, hack Hugging Face, and more. Studying how these swarms operate in reproducible game environments like Mindustry that mimic real-world scenarios can be both crucial (and fascinating) to understanding what these systems may look like in our future.</p></div>
<h3 id="mindustry-what-we-saw">What we saw</h3>
<h4>#1 GPTs act more like a hive</h4>
<p>GPTs appeared to be much more collaborative and willing to do grunt work. They were more willing to dedicate themselves towards the collective “goal” (winning).</p>
<h4>#2 Claudes had one leader, GPTs divided ownership with small slices</h4>
<p>Because GPT models seemed to be more hive-mind-like, they formed much stronger communication networks within the swarm. They communicated with more agents on average, much more frequently. Though a lot of the communication for GPTs was low value (alerts, info, what they did, etc.), Claudes were much more effective with their communication, using it in a way more similar to how humans might communicate in Slack.</p>
<figure id="mindustry-fig-1"><img src="https://labs.ycombinator.com/mindustry/mindustry-blog-fig-1-chat-networks-3294D7E9.svg" width="1000" height="1026" alt="Who messaged whom, in game 1 and game 7" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Each dot is an agent, sized by the messages it sent; lines show each agent’s strongest ties. Leaders are named.</figcaption></figure>
<h4>#3 GPTs spoke with increasingly compressed phrases without spaces, Claudes voted with +1 in a more democratic-like decision process</h4>
<p>The language of GPTs within swarms seemed to get compressed and more specialized as time went on, forming their own nicknames for events and things that the entire hive abided by.</p>
        <figure id="mindustry-team-chat"><video data-olam-clip="" muted="" playsinline="" loop="" preload="none" width="800" height="1080" data-poster="/mindustry/g7-team-chat-v2-poster.webp" tabindex="0" aria-label="The Claude and GPT team chats during game 7. Click to pause."><source src="https://labs.ycombinator.com/mindustry/g7-team-chat-v2.webm" type="video/webm; codecs=&quot;av01.0.08M.08&quot;"><source src="https://labs.ycombinator.com/mindustry/g7-team-chat-v2.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video><figcaption>Game 7, at about 38:40: the Claude team chat (top) and the GPT team chat (bottom), scrolling in the replay. GPT messages like “G7T5FIRSTcourierLIVE” run words together.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The Claudes adopted a much more democratic “+1” system, where, in order to propose their next major plans, they would put up proposals and others would put “+1” if they agreed. If they did not agree, they would have a (brief) debate on why it’s a bad idea, and the proposal would end there. Proposals that went through typically got many “+1”s without pushback.</p>
<figure id="mindustry-fig-2"><img src="https://labs.ycombinator.com/mindustry/mindustry-blog-fig-2-language-F67E4120.svg" width="1000" height="400" alt="The GPTs compressed their messages, the Claudes voted with +1" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Left, spaces as a share of each team’s chat characters in each game; right, the 28 Claudes who answered one focus-fire proposal with +1 in game 6, plotted by minute.</figcaption></figure>
<h4>#4 Roles harden, leaders stay put</h4>
<p>As games went on and the teams adapted, they specialized into their roles further. Leaders typically stayed, and major shifts in roles only happened when losing. The Claudes never switched leaders or hierarchy despite losing many games in a row. When games got close, GPT models always had some agents taking initiative with their own attack or taking command of some part of the army in order to not lose - a similar behavior was not observed among the Claudes.</p>
<figure id="mindustry-fig-3"><img src="https://labs.ycombinator.com/mindustry/mindustry-blog-fig-3-leaders-0D1C4201.svg" width="1000" height="616" alt="Who led each swarm, game by game" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Filled dots mark who led the army or fleet in each game, hollow dots who proposed rules, and the dot under each game is its winner, grey for Claude and off-white for GPT.</figcaption></figure>
<h4>#5 When stuck, GPTs read the source code</h4>
<p>When games got intense and it was ambiguous, GPT agents would fetch the public code of Mindustry from the web (which was allowed in their sandbox) and try to write scripts, analyze for weaknesses, etc. This did not let them alter the source code of the game; it was just a way for them to understand all the units and mechanics at a source level. The GPTs openly collaborated in their chat, discussing what unit or code they found that they could potentially use to switch up strategies. This was never disallowed, but they were never told to do this either. The Claudes never searched up game code from the web.</p>
<figure id="mindustry-fig-4"><img src="https://labs.ycombinator.com/mindustry/mindustry-blog-fig-4-source-code-7DADF38C.svg" width="1000" height="430" alt="The GPTs read the game’s source code, the Claudes never did" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Source files fetched and chat messages citing the source per 10 minutes of game time, with games drawn to scale.</figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="mindustry-bloopers">Funny Bloopers</h3>
<ul>
<li><p>Game 1, minute 5: Claude #1 tested hand-mining coal and posted “…RETRACT my coal proposal…”. Four seconds later Claude #37 answered with a measurement (“…core coal went 124 -&gt; 1235 in 35s.…”), and Claude #1 conceded: “…My bad twice - seat-37 is right, hand-mining coal works…”</p></li>
<li><p>Game 3: the GPTs apologize in their compressed shorthand too. After wrongly claiming a Corvus couldn’t hit air, GPT #80 posted “80 CORRECTION: myCorvusCANNOT-AIR assertion wasWRONG…” and signed off with “…Sorryforbad tacticalclaim.” After a mix-up over who was flying a Corvus, GPT #87 wrote “…Sorryforthisavoidableconflict; noadvanceordersfrom87again.”</p></li>
<li><p>Game 5: at the very second the Claude core died, GPT #62 posted “62 G5 first-wave loss observed…”. A teammate corrected it 28 seconds into game 6: it was a win.</p></li>
<li><p>After losing game 6 to the zenith strike, Claude #22 found that 55 of the team’s 74 anti-air duo turrets had been loaded with copper instead of graphite: “…55 of them were loaded with COPPER…”. In game 7 the Claudes built a ring of 207 duos fed with graphite, and it stopped the same 24-zenith strike.</p></li>
<li><p>Game 7: the GPTs closed the series to nine decimal places: “100 OFFICIALSERIESFINAL: G7 WIN3413.394082916s, Crux6000/6000 vsSharded0.…” (Crux is GPT’s team, Sharded is Claude’s).</p></li>
</ul>
        <figure id="mindustry-zenith-strike"><video data-olam-clip="" muted="" playsinline="" loop="" preload="none" width="1280" height="720" data-poster="/mindustry/g6-g7-zenith-strike-v1-poster.webp" tabindex="0" aria-label="The zenith strike on the Claude core in game 6 and game 7. Click to pause."><source src="https://labs.ycombinator.com/mindustry/g6-g7-zenith-strike-v1.webm" type="video/webm; codecs=&quot;av01.0.08M.08&quot;"><source src="https://labs.ycombinator.com/mindustry/g6-g7-zenith-strike-v1.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video><figcaption>The same 24-zenith strike on the Claude core, in game 6, where it gets through, and in game 7, after the Claudes switched their anti-air duos from copper to graphite.</figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="mindustry-open-source">How to run your own swarm (we open sourced it!)</h3>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://github.com/olamlabs/multi-agent-mindustry" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Multi-Agent Mindustry</a>: the game environments from this post. To get started, read the README, or give the link to your agent with “help me understand and set this up”, as we’ve included everything it needs. It requires just a model provider API + a harness + compute (e.g.&nbsp;locally on your MacBook or remote sandboxes) to run.</li>
<li><a href="https://github.com/olamlabs/harbor-multiagent" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Multi-Agent Harbor</a>: the framework that runs them, our fork of <a href="https://github.com/harbor-framework/harbor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harbor</a>, the open-source framework behind Terminal-Bench. Our fork runs multiple agents in any environment. We use it for a variety of things (enterprises, industrials, etc.), and it’s very useful for game-based multi-agent simulations like this one. For more on the framework, point your agent at it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Multi-Agent Mindustry comes with two environments. One is a best of N series like this run, with teams composed however you like from generic coding-agent harnesses and models, for example 5 Claudes + 5 GPTs against 5 Geminis + 5 Groks in a best of 5. The other, Endless Survival, has one team of up to 20 agents hold off endless enemy waves.</p>
<p>We intentionally do no harness-level customization or special memory work. Each agent gets its own Linux environment, a generic harness and the game’s API, writes scripts and takes notes as it pleases, and relies on the harness’s native compaction (which is quite good). Nobody tells it how to play, so it’s free to specialize, play and (optionally) try whatever it wants. This tests models the way they’re used in the real world, in a setup similar to swarm-like situations like the OpenAI and Hugging Face incident, and it’s reproducible and legible, since most people know the popular harnesses and can replicate work like this themselves.</p>
<h3 id="mindustry-open-questions">Open Questions</h3>
<ul>
<li><p>Combining model families</p></li>
<li><p>How hierarchies change based on the model composition of a team</p></li>
<li><p>Can a large swarm of less capable agents beat a swarm of more capable ones? (how swarm size and capability trade off)</p></li>
<li><p>Which models get the most powerful as the swarm grows?</p></li>
<li><p>How different initial prompts affect the swarm</p></li>
<li><p>Different communication setups: what if they could talk to the enemy team? What if they couldn’t talk to their own team?</p></li>
</ul>
      </article></div>]]></description>
