<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Training</h3><a id="user-content-training" aria-label="Permalink: Training" href="#training"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">First, agents play the real game and every step is recorded as one example (frame, action, next frame, reward), 1–3 million per game. Some episodes start from unusual states, like bricks with holes or random positions, so the data covers more of the game.</p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/blob/main/docs/media/explain_data.gif"><img src="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/raw/main/docs/media/explain_data.gif" alt="data" data-animated-image=""></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Then the world model learns to predict the next frame with a per-cell cross-entropy loss: 30k steps, with the first 3–4k steps run in float, then int8 rounding switches on (<a href="#quantization-and-the-pico">QAT</a>).</p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/blob/main/docs/media/explain_training.gif"><img src="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/raw/main/docs/media/explain_training.gif" alt="training" data-animated-image=""></a></p>
<p dir="auto">The int8 weights are written into Rust source with every float stored as its exact bits. We check them with <code>cargo test</code> to reproduces the Python integer spec bit for bit, and then the firmware runs on the Pico.</p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/blob/main/docs/media/explain_deploy.gif"><img src="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/raw/main/docs/media/explain_deploy.gif" alt="deploy" data-animated-image=""></a></p>
<p dir="auto">An agent trained in the world model reaches states the recorded data never had, so record real games with that agent and fine-tune the world model on everything.</p>
<p dir="auto">The agent in different games stated exploiting them so those are some fixes to it:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>Weighting the loss by "this cell changed" biased the model toward predicting change, so it drew two balls and broke two bricks. Weighting by where things can happen in the input frame, plus the global singleton choice, fixed it.</li>
<li>A Breakout agent in the dream got 154 points with 26 bricks left, because the world model didn't remove bricks from the corner when it were hit. Doom-lite agents learned to snipe from 5 cells away.</li>
<li>Agents trained only in the dream scored 210 there (the real maximum is 56) and 28 in the real game, as <em>World Models</em> (Ha &amp; Schmidhuber) predicts. Each collect-and-fine-tune round closed the exploit that agent had found.</li>
<li>The Doom-lite dream sometimes lost the enemy sprite and never drew it again: the model copied the previous view instead of redrawing it from the minimap. Noise augmentation fixed it (<code>train --noise 0.3</code>, as in GameNGen): input views get random pixels and sometimes lose the sprite.</li>
<li><code>d=32</code> couldn't absorb the wider Breakout data and <code>d=48</code> could. Pac-Man's 48×48 frame needed <code>d=64</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">The agents: stream-x</h2><a id="user-content-the-agents-stream-x" aria-label="Permalink: The agents: stream-x" href="#the-agents-stream-x"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/blob/main/docs/media/explain_streamx.gif"><img src="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/raw/main/docs/media/explain_streamx.gif" alt="stream-x" data-animated-image=""></a></p>
<p dir="auto">The agents use Stream AC(λ) (Elsayed, Vasan &amp; Mahmood, 2024), which learns from one transition at a time: no replay buffer, no batches, no target network. Eligibility traces carry credit back in time, and ObGD shrinks any step that would overshoot the TD target. The policy and value networks each have two hidden layers of 64 units (32 on the Pico) with LayerNorm.</p>
<p dir="auto">The input is a feature vector read off the frame (<code>training/streamx.py: observe_*</code>, mirrored in Rust in <code>tinyworldformer-rs/src/agent.rs</code>). For example, the ball's row, column and heading in Breakout, or in Pac-Man, for each direction, the shortest-path distance to the nearest pellet and how close each ghost is.</p>
