<p dir="auto">Without shaping, Doom-lite agents camp in the start corner and fire, because the enemy always comes to them. <code>--idle-penalty 0.1</code> costs 0.1 for each step spent in a cell visited more than 6 times in the last 20 steps. The position is read off the minimap, so the penalty works inside the dream too. With it the agent patrols and gets twice the kills.</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>tinyworldformer-rs/src/learner.rs</code> is the same algorithm in <code>no_std</code> Rust, with the backward pass written by hand. It matches 200 PyTorch updates to within 3.5e-6. On the host, a fresh agent inside the Rust int8 Flappy Bird dream averages 6 pipes per episode after 25k steps, 121 after 100k, and 300+ after 250k.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Quantization and the Pico</h2><a id="user-content-quantization-and-the-pico" aria-label="Permalink: Quantization and the Pico" href="#quantization-and-the-pico"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/blob/main/docs/media/explain_quantize.gif"><img src="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/raw/main/docs/media/explain_quantize.gif" alt="quantization" data-animated-image=""></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Every matrix in the world model is int8 with one scale per output row (<code>scale = max|w| / 127</code>). Activations are int8 too, with one scale per tensor and per loop index, since the loops see different ranges. Matmuls sum int8 × int8 into int32 (two multiply-adds per <code>SMLAD</code> instruction on the RP2350) and then apply one float multiply by the two scales. Softmax uses a 1024-entry exp table, with probabilities stored as uint8. Norms, residuals and embeddings stay in float32, since the RP2350 has an FPU.</p>
<p dir="auto">Training applies the same rounding (QAT with a straight-through estimator), so the int8 model is the model that was trained. <code>rustexport/intref.py</code> is the int8 model in numpy with every float operation in a fixed order; the Rust engine reproduces it bit for bit, and <code>cargo test</code> checks golden vectors for every exported env.</p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/blob/main/docs/media/sizes.png"><img src="https://github.com/SheepSeb/tinyworldformer/raw/main/docs/media/sizes.png" alt="sizes"></a></p>
