<p dir="auto">Flash and RAM are measured from the firmware ELF (<code>size -A</code>), built with the Display Pack driver, so they include the code, USB, the display driver and the on-device learner. The Pico 2 W has 4 MB of flash and 520 KB of SRAM, 512 KiB of it in the main memory map. The float32 embedding tables are the largest part and could be int8 too.</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>src/tinyworldformer/rustexport/</code> imports only numpy and torch (a test keeps it that way), so other projects can reuse the export: build a model from <code>rustexport.qat</code> (<code>QLinear</code>, <code>LoopedBlock</code>), train it with <code>ActQuant.enabled = True</code> after a float warm-up, convert it with <code>rustexport.convert.looped_model</code>, then write the Rust source with <code>int_model_arrays</code>, <code>mlp_arrays</code> and <code>codegen.write_module</code>. <code>rustexport.Golden</code> writes the vectors a Rust test checks.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">On the chip</h2><a id="user-content-on-the-chip" aria-label="Permalink: On the chip" href="#on-the-chip"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">The firmware (<code>tinyworldformer-rs/firmware</code>) is <code>no_std</code> Rust on embassy. Nothing is allocated at run time: the weights are <code>static</code> arrays in flash, and every buffer is a fixed-size <code>static</code> in RAM.</p>
<p dir="auto">Core 1 runs the game. One pass of its loop (<code>dream_loop</code> in <code>firmware/src/main.rs</code>) is one frame:</p>
<ol dir="auto">
<li>The actor reads features off the current frame (<code>agent::observe</code>) and runs its MLP to pick a button. If on-device learning is on, the learning agent picks instead; if you are pressing buttons, your press is used.</li>
<li>The int8 transformer runs 2 loops over the frame and the button (<code>Engine::forward</code>), and decoding turns its scores into the next frame: background argmax per cell, each singleton placed once (sampled for random events with an xorshift RNG), then the sprite stamps. The reward is the argmax of the reward head; lives and episode ends are counters on top.</li>
<li>If learning is on, the learner updates from this one transition and then forgets it.</li>
<li>The frame goes to core 0 through a two-slot channel. If core 0 is still busy with the previous frame, this one is dropped, so core 1 never waits for the screen.</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto">When you play, core 1 slows down to a playable speed (66–150 ms per frame, depending on the game). The agent runs as fast as the chip allows.</p>
<p dir="auto">Core 0 runs embassy async tasks for everything else. Over USB serial, each frame goes out as coloured ANSI text with a status line (model time, actor time, fps, env and agent names), and keys come back in. The optional Display Pack (<code>--features display</code>) is a 320×240 ST7789 over SPI at up to 62.5 MHz, and only cells that changed since the last frame are redrawn. Its buttons are polled every 20 ms. The two cores share only the frame channel and a few atomics: who is playing, the pressed key, learning on/off, and the loop count.</p>
<p dir="auto">Where the memory goes:</p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>in flash</th>
<th>in RAM</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>world model</td>
<td>int8 matrices, float32 embeddings, norms and scales, the game's opening frames and colours (52–138 KB)</td>
<td>scratch buffers for one forward pass: activations for 36–80 tokens, the int8 copies the matmuls read, and the per-cell scores of the next frame (114 KB for Flappy Bird up to 368 KB for Pac-Man, whose per-cell scores alone take 184 KB)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>actor</td>
<td>float32 MLP weights and feature normalization (51–61 KB)</td>
<td>its features and hidden layers (about 1 KB)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>on-device learner</td>
<td>code only</td>
<td>weights, eligibility traces and normalization for two 32-unit networks (49 KB for Pac-Man up to 106 KB for Doom-lite; it scales with the number of input features)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>firmware</td>
<td>code, USB, display driver (about 150 KB)</td>
<td>stacks (16 KB for core 1), USB buffers, frame channels</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir="auto">Every game fits in the 4 MB of flash and 512 KiB of SRAM. Breakout uses 361 KB of flash and 299 KB of SRAM; Pac-Man, the largest, uses 600 KB and 460 KB.</p>
<p dir="auto">Most of the time goes into int8 dot products (<code>dot_i8</code> in <code>src/lib.rs</code>). One 32-bit load fetches 4 weights, <code>SXTB16</code> unpacks bytes 0/2 and 1/3 into 16-bit halves, and <code>SMLAD</code> does two multiply-adds in one instruction. Attention multiplies uint8 probabilities by int8 values, so it uses <code>UXTB16</code> instead. Norms, the exp table lookup and the final per-output scale stay in float32 on the FPU. A frame at 2 loops is 2–4M multiply-adds (8.2M for Pac-Man); on a desktop CPU the same Rust code dreams a frame in 0.7 ms (Flappy Bird) to 1.5 ms (Breakout). I haven't measured the frame rate on the Pico itself yet.</p>
<p dir="auto">The board boots with a fresh agent learning on the device. Press <code>l</code> over USB, or hold B for 1 s on the Display Pack, to switch to the pretrained actor, and again to switch back.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Flashing a game and an agent</h2><a id="user-content-flashing-a-game-and-an-agent" aria-label="Permalink: Flashing a game and an agent" href="#flashing-a-game-and-an-agent"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Every export sits side by side in the crate: world models in <code>tinyworldformer-rs/models/envs/&lt;env&gt;/</code>, agents in <code>models/agents/&lt;game&gt;/&lt;agent&gt;/</code>. You pick both at build time.</p>
